              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 5,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": 3,\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 2,\n  \"clarity\": 4\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 3
            },
            "ref_text": "The paper proposed to encode text into a binary matrix by using a compressing code for each word in each matrix row. The idea is interesting, and overall introduction is clear.\n\nThis paper proposes a new character encoding scheme for use with character-convolutional language models. This is a poor quality paper, is unclear in the results (what metric is even reported in Table 6), and has little significance (though this may highlight the opportunity to revisit the encoding scheme for characters).",
            "test_text": "- CLAR: The writing quality is poor, with frequent grammatical errors, awkward phrasing, and inconsistent terminology that hinder readability and professional presentation. Evidence anchors: SRC-001, SRC-003, SRC-011.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 1: Perform a thorough line-by-line proofreading to correct grammatical errors such as 'reduces drastically' and 'In all of ours experiments'.\n\n- CLAR: The algorithm presentation lacks formal pseudocode, relying instead on prose and examples, which makes the encoding procedure less precise and harder to reproduce without ambiguity. Evidence anchors: SRC-003, SRC-004.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 2: Provide a clear, step-by-step pseudocode block for the encoding algorithm, explicitly defining inputs, outputs, and edge cases such as the handling of characters not in the rank list.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 4: Expand the abbreviations in Table 6 or provide a clear legend, and specify the type of merge operation (e.g., concatenation, addition) used in the network architecture.\n\n- CLAR: The paper lacks a clear definition of the 'compressing inspired techniques' and does not explicitly state the name of the algorithm (Tagged Huffman Code) until the Discussion section, which delays the reader's understanding of the core contribution. Evidence anchors: SRC-001, SRC-011.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 5: Introduce the specific compression technique (Tagged Huffman Code) in the Introduction or early in Section 2 and explain the connection between the proposed encoding and Huffman coding more clearly."
          },
          "QUAL-CMP": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": 3,
              "alignment": 4,
              "average": 3.3333333333333335,
              "claim_extraction": "claim",
              "clarity": 4,
              "completeness": 3,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 3,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": 2,\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 3,\n  \"clarity\": 4\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 2
            },
            "ref_text": "However, the work lacks justification for this particular way of encoding, and no comparison for any other encoding mechanism is provided except for the one-hot encoding used in Zhang & LeCun 2015. The results using this particular encoding are not better than any previous work.\n\nOverall, the lack of comparisons and the questionable choices for the networks render this work lacking significance to be published in ICLR 2018.",
            "test_text": "- QUAL: The comparison is limited to the 2015 Zhang & LeCun baseline and traditional non-deep learning methods, lacking comparison against more recent or state-of-the-art character-level CNN variants or other efficient encoding methods that have emerged since 2015. Evidence anchors: SRC-008.\n\n- Suggested revision for QUAL opinion 3: Include comparisons with more recent character-level CNN architectures or other efficient embedding techniques to better contextualize the proposed method's performance.\n\n- Suggested revision for ORIG opinion 2: More rigorously justify why this specific compression scheme is superior to other dimensionality reduction techniques (e.g., hashing, PCA) for CNN inputs, beyond just parameter count."
          },
